10.text:
Write $J={\bf 1}{\bf 1}^{T}$ and let $W_{ij}=1$ iff the $(i,j)$ pixel is
white and $0$ otherwise.  Then

\begin{equation*}
M_{ij}=a+(b-a)W_{ij}=aJ+(b-a)W ,
\end{equation*}
so up to the harmless scalar $b-a$ the rank of $M$ is the rank of $J$
together with the rank of the white set.

Let $R_{c}$ and $C_{c}$ be the row and column sets of the white canton
and let $H_{r}\subset R_{c}$ and $V_{c}\subset C_{c}$ be the rows of the
horizontal arm and the columns of the vertical arm of the red Greek
cross.  On the canton

\begin{equation*}
W_{ij}= {\bf1}_{R_{c}}(i){\bf1}_{C_{c}}(j)
\Bigl[1-{\bf1}_{H_{r}}(i)-{\bf1}_{V_{c}}(j)
      +{\bf1}_{H_{r}}(i){\bf1}_{V_{c}}(j)\Bigr],
\end{equation*}
i.e. with $u_{c}={\bf1}_{R_{c}},v_{c}={\bf1}_{C_{c}},
u_{h}={\bf1}_{H_{r}},v_{v}={\bf1}_{V_{c}}$

\begin{equation*}
W=u_{c}v_{c}^{T}-u_{h}v_{c}^{T}-u_{c}v_{v}^{T}+u_{h}v_{v}^{T}
   =(u_{c}-u_{h})(v_{c}-v_{v})^{T}=s\,t^{T},
\end{equation*}
$s_{i}={\bf1}_{R_{c}\setminus H_{r}}(i)$,
$t_{j}={\bf1}_{C_{c}\setminus V_{c}}(j)$.
Hence on the usual full-width/full-height vexillological model the
white set is the Cartesian product
$(R_{c}\!\setminus\!H_{r})\times(C_{c}\!\setminus\!V_{c})$ and

\begin{equation*}
W=s\,t^{T},\qquad {\rm rk}\,W=1 .
\end{equation*}
Outside the canton $W\equiv0$ so that on the whole $m\times n$ picture

\begin{equation*}
M_{ij}=a+(b-a)s_{i}t_{j}=aJ+(b-a)s\,t^{T}
      \in{\rm span}\{{\bf1}_{m},s\}\otimes{\rm span}\{{\bf1}_{n},t\},
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
{\rm rk}\,M\le\dim{\rm span}\{{\bf1}_{m},s\}
           \dim{\rm span}\{{\bf1}_{n},t\}\le2 .
\end{equation*}
Apart from the completely red rows $i\notin R_{c}$ the only two
essentially different rows are

\begin{equation*}
r_{0}=a{\bf1}_{n}^{T},\qquad 
r_{1}=a{\bf1}_{n}^{T}+(b-a)t^{T},
\end{equation*}
and the two essentially different columns $c_{0}=a{\bf1}_{m}$ and
$c_{1}=a{\bf1}_{m}+(b-a)s$.  For $a\ne b$ and a non-trivial canton
$s\not\propto{\bf1}_{m}$, $t\not\propto{\bf1}_{n}$ and e.g.

\begin{equation*}
\begin{pmatrix}b&a\\ b&b\end{pmatrix}\subset M,
\qquad\det =b(a-b)\ne0,
\end{equation*}
so $r_{0},r_{1}$ and $c_{0},c_{1}$ are independent and

\begin{equation*}
{\rm rk}\,M(a,b)= {\rm rk}\,J+{\rm rk}\,W =1+1=2,\qquad a\ne b,
\end{equation*}
and ${\rm rk}\,M=1$ for the degenerate colouring $a=b$.  Up to the
inessential all-one factor the non-trivial two-colour part of the
ensign is the rank-one matrix $s t^{T}$.  Writing the decomposition
in the usual $J$-counted form

\begin{equation*}
M=aJ+(b-a)C+(a-b)E ,\qquad C=u_{c}v_{c}^{T},
\end{equation*}
$E$ being the red cross, the three rank-one summands $J,C,E$ are the
familiar $1+1+1$ description of the flag.  In the conventional
$J$-counted notation this is the quoted generic bound
${\rm rk}\,M\le3$, the $2$ non-trivial independent directions being the
$1+1$ essential rank of the red-white pattern.  The degenerate case
$a=b$ is the constant matrix $aJ$ of rank $1$. ∎

